clark county school district principals  already thinking about how to reopen  campuses  the first step to welcoming back some  students in small groups as approved  last week by board trustees 8 news now  reporter kristen drummond speaks with  some principals about their potential  plans and challenges  everybody's circumstances is so unique  as we go through this uncharted  territory  ryan lewis prepares to navigate a new  situation  the gerheim elementary school principal  considers his approach to inviting  some students back on campus in small  groups i like the fact that  we are getting to to tailor it to the  needs of our kids and our communities to  a certain extent  giving principals flexibility when  creating their plans  from the number of children on campus to  who comes back  lewis wants to prioritize special needs  students those  not engaging in classes or are falling  behind  meanwhile principal tam larnard at  spring valley high school  chooses to partly focus on seniors we  want to make sure that  that the circumstances don't prevent  them from reaching their goal and  graduating from high school  but larnard and lewis question certain  issues that includes the district not  providing transportation  the overwhelming majority of my students  are not in walking distance what are the  challenges you've identified at your  elementary school  so the big challenge is just staffing  quite honestly uncertain about how many  teachers may voluntarily come back  principals must also decide when to host  the small groups  it is very possible that students will  have another teacher to serve them  so in my mind it may be more of an after  school thing principal larnard wants to  start surveying teachers this week  regional superintendents must sign off  on each school's plan  kristin drummond 8 news now
 

